


ENSONIQPLAY(1)        FGX Programmer's Manual        ENSONIQPLAY(1)



NAME
     ensoniqplay - play PCM audio through the Ensoniq 5503 DOC

SYNOPSIS
     ensoniqplay [ -r rate ] [ -s | -u | -a ] file
     ensoniqplay [ -r rate ] [ -s | -u ] -

DESCRIPTION
     Ensoniqplay plays a PCM sample through the Apple IIGS
     Ensoniq 5503 "DOC" sound chip.  It accepts WAV files and
     raw 8-bit PCM, and reads from a file or, when the argument
     is a single dash, from standard input.

     Input is converted to the 8-bit unsigned samples the DOC
     plays natively: 16-bit WAV is reduced to 8-bit, stereo is
     mixed to mono, and a source faster than the chip's ceiling
     of about 26320 Hz is decimated to fit so the pitch stays
     correct.

     How the sample reaches the chip depends on its size.  One
     that fits the DOC's 32 KB wavetable is uploaded once and
     played by a single oscillator (a "placed" sample).  A
     larger one is streamed: it is fed to a wavetable double-
     buffer under the sound interrupt while a second, silent
     oscillator locked to the playback rate drives the refills.
     Streaming from standard input works the same way, filling a
     RAM ring from the pipe as the chip drains it, so playback
     needs neither the whole input in memory nor a file it can
     seek.

FILE FORMATS
     WAV          RIFF/WAVE, 8-bit (unsigned) or 16-bit
                  (signed), mono or stereo, any sample rate --
                  every parameter comes from the header.

     raw          Headerless 8-bit mono.  The sample rate is set
                  by -r (default 22050 Hz) and the signedness by
                  the options below.  Standard input is always
                  raw.

OPTIONS
     -r rate      Sample rate in Hz for raw input (default
                  22050).  Ignored for WAV, which carries its
                  own rate.

     -u           Treat raw input as 8-bit unsigned: the default,
                  the DOC-native form, silence at 128.

     -s           Treat raw input as 8-bit signed (two's
                  complement, silence at 0).

     -a           Autodetect raw signedness from the sample
                  histogram.  Reliable for recordings, which
                  rest at their quiet level, but ambiguous for
                  full-scale tones, so it is not the default.
                  Unavailable on standard input, which cannot be
                  rewound.

EXAMPLES
     ensoniqplay drum.wav
                  play a WAV file.

     ensoniqplay -r 11025 voice.pcm
                  play raw 8-bit unsigned PCM at 11025 Hz.

     ensoniqplay -s amiga.raw
                  play raw signed 8-bit PCM.

     cat song.pcm | ensoniqplay -
                  stream raw 8-bit PCM from a pipe.

NOTES
     The player claims the DOC while it runs, so the system
     silences the chip if the program is killed and audio keeps
     playing when the player is backgrounded.

     8-bit signed and unsigned data are the same bytes with a
     different zero point, so a raw file with no quiet passages
     is indistinguishable between the two; give -s or -u when -a
     guesses wrong.

BUGS
     Streamed samples can carry an audible buzz under emulation
     (MAME, KEGS): writing the wavetable while the chip reads it
     perturbs the oscillators there in a way real hardware does
     not.  Placed samples are unaffected.

     Standard input is raw 8-bit only; a WAV piped in is not
     parsed as a header.



